m Warehouse A to B at 30 km/h and then returns from B to A at 60 km/h. Hmm, average speed can be tricky because it's not just the average of the two speeds. I remember that when dealing with round trips, the average speed is the total distance divided by the total time.\n\nLet me break it down. Let's assume the distance between A and B is D kilometers. So the total round trip distance is 2D. Now, I need to calculate the time taken for each leg of the trip. \n\nFor the first part, going from A to B at 30 km/h, the time taken would be distance divided by speed, so D/30 hours. Then, returning from B to A at 60 km/h, the time would be D/60 hours. The total time for the trip is the sum of these two times: D/30 + D/60. \n\nTo add those fractions, I need a common denominator. The common denominator for 30 and 60 is 60. So converting D/30 to 2D/60, the total time becomes 2D/60 + D/60 = 3D/60 = D/20 hours. \n\nNow, average speed is total distance divided by total time. The total distance is 2D, and the total time is D/20. So average speed = 2D / (D/20). The D cancels out, leaving 2 * 20 = 40 km/h. \n\nWait, let me check that again. If the distance is D each way, total distance is 2D. Time for each way is D/30 and D/60. Adding those gives D/30 + D/60. Let me compute that numerically. Let's say D is 60 km for simplicity. Then time to go is 60/30 = 2 hours, time to return is 60/60 = 1 hour. Total time is 3 hours. Total distance is 120 km. Average speed is 120/3 = 40 km/h. Yep, that checks out. So the answer should be 40 km/h.\n</think>\n\n\\boxed{40}<|im_end|>", "stop_reason": "eos_token", "completed": true, "excursion_token_indices": [80, 128, 244, 273, 381, 426, 508], "excursion_count": 7, "rejected_return_token_indices": [], "recurrent_block_calls": 2286, "wall_seconds": 70.5811}, "sha256": "92e8b57fd2da5a4a241d3c67ee04f07c991f027e2c9f6418d876a1f6a27e2f05"}